From: Marcus Ramberg <marcus@means.no>
Subject: [PATCH] pinctrl: qcom: lpass-lpi: defer probe when the clocks time out

The LPASS LPI pinctrl core and audio clocks are provided by the ADSP,
through q6prm over GLINK. If the pinctrl driver probes before the ADSP
remoteproc has booted, enabling them does not return -EPROBE_DEFER: the
clock enable request goes unanswered and times out with -ETIMEDOUT.

The clocks are now handled through pm_clk/runtime PM, so nothing in probe
touches them and the failure only surfaces later, on the first register
access, as a hard failure -- the pinctrl device never probes again even
once the ADSP is up. Everything downstream of it stays in deferred probe
forever, which on a Fairphone 5 leaves the whole sound card unusable.

Resume the device once during probe and translate the timeout into
-EPROBE_DEFER, so the probe is retried after the ADSP has booted.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Marcus Ramberg <marcus@means.no>

---
diff --git a/drivers/pinctrl/qcom/pinctrl-lpass-lpi.c b/drivers/pinctrl/qcom/pinctrl-lpass-lpi.c
index 5fd4a4eba654..4dcae85288b8 100644
--- a/drivers/pinctrl/qcom/pinctrl-lpass-lpi.c
+++ b/drivers/pinctrl/qcom/pinctrl-lpass-lpi.c
@@ -545,6 +545,22 @@ int lpi_pinctrl_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
+	/*
+	 * The core/audio clocks are provided by the ADSP (through q6prm over
+	 * GLINK). If the ADSP has not booted yet, the clock enable request
+	 * times out instead of deferring. Resume once here so the timeout can
+	 * be turned into a probe deferral, instead of failing later on the
+	 * first register access and leaving the whole sound card stuck in
+	 * deferred probe.
+	 */
+	ret = pm_runtime_resume_and_get(dev);
+	if (ret) {
+		if (ret == -ETIMEDOUT)
+			ret = -EPROBE_DEFER;
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, ret, "Can't enable clocks\n");
+	}
+	pm_runtime_put_autosuspend(dev);
+
 	pctrl->desc.pctlops = &lpi_gpio_pinctrl_ops;
 	pctrl->desc.pmxops = &lpi_gpio_pinmux_ops;
 	pctrl->desc.confops = &lpi_gpio_pinconf_ops;
